## The split, stated before the test list

PRD AC-8 is the unit's only DOM-bound criterion, and
`documentation/40-engineering/testing-strategy.md` keeps UI verification manual
by deliberate choice — this project's only test tier is Vitest inside workerd,
which has no DOM. `docs/context/methodology.md` does not accept that as a
blanket exemption; it imposes an obligation: **extract the decidable part into
`src/shared` and test it first, and only the thin adapter is exempt.**

Applied here, the decidable part is the whole of the edit semantics — which keys
a patch carries, clearing versus omitting, and date exclusivity. That is what
this suite covers. What remains manual is genuinely thin: click to enter edit
mode, Enter/blur/Escape, panel open and close.

## Test files written this session

- `.worktrees/task-detail-and-dates/test/task-edit.test.ts` — 25 cases in five
  `describe` blocks against the new pure `src/shared/task-edit.ts`:
  - **`dateModeOf`** — deadline, scheduled, and neither.
  - **only what changed** — an unchanged draft yields `{}`; a single edit yields
    exactly one key; a changed title is trimmed; a whitespace-only retitle
    yields `{}` (an empty title and an empty body are both 400s, and the owner
    should meet neither); a title differing only by surrounding whitespace is
    omitted.
  - **clearing versus omitting** — description and priority each cleared with an
    explicit `null` when they had a value, and omitted entirely when they did
    not. This is the client half of PRD AC-2 and the pair of cases that a naive
    `?? null` implementation fails.
  - **date exclusivity** — switching modes emits only the new mode's key and
    explicitly asserts the other is absent; `No date` clears whichever date the
    Task carried; an undated Task staying undated emits nothing; setting a first
    date works; an unchanged date is omitted; three invalid values (empty,
    `2026-02-31`, `01/09/2026`) emit no date key at all; and a loop over all
    three modes asserts both dates are never set together.
  - **editable-set ceiling** — a maximal edit emits exactly
    `description`, `priority`, `scheduledDate`, `title` and nothing else.

## Why this suite is legitimately RED before implementation

Executed against the phase-3 worktree state, the suite fails to load:

```
Error: Cannot find module '../src/shared/task-edit'
```

For a brand-new pure module this is the only red available, and unlike phase 3
there is no older implementation to demonstrate a behavioural red against — the
module *is* the unit under test. Recorded plainly rather than dressed up.

What carries the discrimination instead is the content of the assertions: the
clearing-versus-omitting pairs (a `null` when the field had a value, absence
when it did not) and the mode-switch pairs (asserting the *other* date key is
absent, not merely that the new one is present) each fail against the obvious
naive implementations — a blanket `?? null`, or a patch builder that emits every
field it knows about. A suite that only asserted "the changed field is present"
would pass against both and prove nothing.

No test asserts on an invented internal, a private field, or a call count. No
mocks are used: the module is pure, so the tests call it directly.

